          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Why does colon carcinoma most commonly metastasise to the liver before other org</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Why does colon carcinoma most commonly metastasise to the liver before other organs?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The liver has the largest capillary bed in the body</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While the liver has an extensive capillary network, the primary reason is the anatomical venous drainage pattern, not capillary density alone.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Colon carcinoma cells express surface receptors specific to hepatocytes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is not the primary mechanism. The anatomical route of venous drainage explains the liver-first pattern of colorectal metastasis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Venous drainage from the colon passes through the portal vein directly to the liver</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The portal venous system carries blood from the colon directly to the liver, making it the first capillary bed that tumour emboli encounter — consistent with Paget's seed-and-soil hypothesis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The liver lacks adequate immune surveillance to destroy tumour cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The liver contains Kupffer cells that perform immune surveillance. The primary explanation is the portal venous drainage route, not an immune deficit.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember Paget's seed-and-soil hypothesis: the portal vein drains the colon directly into the liver, making it the first capillary bed that tumour emboli encounter."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Understanding portal venous drainage explains why colorectal cancers characteristically present with hepatic metastases, often before any other distant site."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
