          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with squamous cell carcinoma of the lung presents with elevated serum </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with squamous cell carcinoma of the lung presents with elevated serum calcium but no evidence of bone metastases on imaging. Which mechanism best explains this finding?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteolytic metastases below the detection threshold of current imaging</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The scenario specifies no bone metastases. Hypercalcaemia in squamous cell lung carcinoma is a paraneoplastic effect, not an occult bony lesion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ectopic secretion of parathyroid hormone-related protein by the tumour</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Squamous cell lung carcinoma secretes PTHrP, which mimics parathyroid hormone and raises serum calcium — a classic paraneoplastic syndrome distinct from bone metastases.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cachexia-induced calcium mobilisation from bone stores</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cachexia involves cytokine-driven muscle wasting and anorexia, not calcium mobilisation. The mechanism here is ectopic hormone secretion by the tumour itself.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Direct invasion of the parathyroid glands by the lung tumour</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A lung mass does not invade the parathyroid glands. This is an ectopic hormone secretion — a paraneoplastic syndrome acting at a distance from the primary tumour.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "This is a classic paraneoplastic syndrome. Squamous cell lung carcinoma secretes PTHrP (parathyroid hormone-related protein), causing hypercalcaemia without any bone involvement."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Squamous cell lung carcinoma secreting PTHrP is one of the most commonly tested paraneoplastic syndromes, alongside small-cell lung carcinoma secreting ACTH and ADH."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
