          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A pathologist receives a poorly differentiated tumour biopsy and needs to determ</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A pathologist receives a poorly differentiated tumour biopsy and needs to determine whether it is a carcinoma or a lymphoma. Which immunohistochemical marker pair would best distinguish between these two possibilities?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Oestrogen receptor and progesterone receptor</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ER and PR guide therapy selection in breast carcinoma but do not distinguish carcinoma from lymphoma. Many carcinomas are ER/PR-negative.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>D2-40 and CD31</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>D2-40 marks lymphatic endothelium and CD31 marks vascular endothelium. These confirm lymphovascular invasion, not tumour cell lineage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cytokeratin and leukocyte common antigen</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Cytokeratin is the epithelial marker identifying carcinomas, while leukocyte common antigen (CD45) identifies lymphoid neoplasms. This is the standard first-line IHC panel for poorly differentiated tumours of uncertain lineage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Alpha-fetoprotein and CA-125</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>AFP and CA-125 are serum tumour markers used for monitoring treatment response and recurrence, not tissue-based immunohistochemical markers for lineage determination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "For tumour lineage determination, cytokeratin identifies carcinomas (epithelial origin) and leukocyte common antigen (LCA/CD45) identifies lymphomas (lymphoid origin). This is the standard first-line IHC panel."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Cytokeratin (epithelial) and LCA/CD45 (lymphoid) are the first-line IHC panel when a poorly differentiated tumour's lineage is unclear. S-100 would be added if melanoma is suspected."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
